<mxfile host="65bd71144e">
    <diagram id="5HOKI8PrhnhP1xh2KtFT" name="Page-1">
        <mxGraphModel dx="1454" dy="812" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="850" pageHeight="1100" math="0" shadow="0">
            <root>
                <mxCell id="0"/>
                <mxCell id="1" parent="0"/>
                <mxCell id="2" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;Heading&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(63, 63, 63); background-color: transparent;&quot;&gt;The Configuration Parameter File (CPF) merge feature in InterSystems IRIS allows you to apply declarative updates, deltas, and object actions to an instance&#39;s configuration. It enables automated deployments, container initialization, and system reconfigurations by injecting settings or running CRUD-based actions upon startup or via the command line.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Based on experiences shared by developers on the InterSystems Developer Community, the deployment strategy for a customer running InterSystems IRIS heavily focuses on Infrastructure as Code (IaC), containerization, and strict separation of code and data.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. Modern Infrastructure as Code (IaC) &amp;amp; Container Strategies&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;For agile, cloud-first, or modernized on-premises setups, developers strongly favor automated container deployments over manual installations. The three primary pathways used are:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;InterSystems Kubernetes Operator (IKO): This is the preferred strategy for managing complex IRIS clusters. Developers use the IKO to automate the deployment, scaling, and orchestration of containerized, sharded database systems.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: pre; white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;InterSystems Cloud Manager (ICM): Developers use this provision tool to deploy and configure IRIS Docker containers into public clouds (like AWS, Azure, GCP) or VMware instances using a Terraform-based infrastructure approach.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: pre; white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;Docker &amp;amp; Docker Compose: For smaller scales or straightforward customer environments, packaging the front-end application, IRIS database, and reporting tools into Docker containers via a unified docker-compose.yml file is common. This guarantees consistency across development, staging, and production environments.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;fontSize=15;" vertex="1" parent="1">
                    <mxGeometry x="10" y="10" width="830" height="660" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
            </root>
        </mxGraphModel>
    </diagram>
</mxfile>